  "texts": {
    "preamble": "Kali Podo Mandal vs State Of Jharkhand on 10 April, 2017",
    "facts": "Since this revision application is pending since 2002, the same is being disposed of based on the materials available on records. Small contents from each of the packets were taken as sample and they were sent to the public analyst. The public analyst submitted a report in which it was stated that the seized salt was adulterated. After obtaining sanction for prosecution from the local authority P.F. Case No. 44 of 1991 was instituted. The prosecution in course of its evidence had examined three witnesses. P.W. - 1, Birendra Kumar Sinha, is the Food Inspector who had visited the shop of the petitioner and had purchased three packets of salt weighing one kilogram each. He has further stated that three Kali Podo Mandal vs State Of Jharkhand on 10 April, 2017 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/139963800/ 1 packets of salt were wrapped separately and the paper which was affixed on it was signed by the petitioner and the purchase slip was also prepared. This witness has further stated that one part of the sample was sent to the public analyst and the rest two packets were sent to the local health authority. It has also stated by this witness that after the salt was found to be adulterated the sanction for prosecution was obtained from local authority and the prosecution report was filed. P.W. - 1 has further stated that the report of the public analyst revealed that there was no iodine in the salt whereas the report of the Central Food Laboratory disclosed otherwise. P.W. - 2, Kayum Ansari, was tendered by the prosecution. P.W.- 3, Fagu Mian, is also said to have accompanied P.W. - 1 and he has also stated in similar terms to what has been stated by P.W. - 1. D.W. - 1, Adalat Gorain and D.W. - 2, Kalipada Mandal had stated that salt was seized from a gunny bag which was kept loose and sealed in bottles. In the trial court a submission was advanced on behalf of the petitioner that the report of the public analyst as well as the report of the Central Food Laboratory contradicted each other. A further submission had been advanced that Section 13 (2) of the Prevention of Food Adulteration Act has been violated. The learned trial court as well as the learned appellate court had appreciated the contentions made on behalf of the petitioner. It appears that on 01.06.1991 the petitioner had surrendered before the trial court and on the same day had prayed that the seized sample be sent to the Central Food Laboratory for its examination which was allowed and accordingly the sample was sent to the Central Food Laboratory and a report was also prepared which was exhibited.",
    "arguments": "",
